# A contrastive negation denies one framing and asserts another
# (`This isn't a cache; it's a ledger`), so its first clause answers a
# claim nobody made. The fix states what the thing is, and gives the
# contrast a sentence of its own only when a reader would expect the
# denied framing.
#
# The semicolon branch takes any words between the negation and the
# semicolon. The comma branch needs a determiner, `about`, or a
# minimizer on both sides of the comma (`isn't about speed, it's about
# correctness`), because a conditional takes the same form with a
# predicate (`If the file isn't found, it's created`), and Weir has no
# lookbehind for the `if`. A sentence that splits the two clauses with
# a period (`It's not about speed. It's about correctness.`) passes,
# because a rule reads one sentence at a time.
#
# Each gap length is its own alternative ending in the punctuation,
# the way the not-only rule writes its `but` tail. A space in a pattern
# is a whitespace token, so each `*` is one word, and the punctuation
# sits glued to the last one.
expr neg [isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't, (is not), (are not), (was not), (were not)]
expr then [it's, they're, (it is), (they are), (it was), (they were)]
expr lead [DET, about, just, only, merely, simply]
expr semi [(*;), (* *;), (* * *;), (* * * *;), (* * * * *;), (* * * * * *;)]
expr comma [(*,), (* *,), (* * *,), (* * * *,), (* * * * *,)]
expr main [(@neg @semi @then), (@neg @lead @comma @then [DET, about])]
let message "Contrastive negation. State what it is, and give the contrast its own sentence only if a reader expects it."
let description "Flags a clause that denies one framing before asserting another, such as this isn't a cache; it's a ledger."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "If the file isn't found, it's created."
allows "The value isn't a number, so the parser rejects it."
allows "The cache is a ledger of every decision the scheduler makes."
